2. Linear Mixed-Effects Models (LMM) for Continuous Longitudinal Outcomes
For continuous neurocognitive and psycholinguistic indices (Latency, Fluency, N400_Amp), Linear Mixed-Effects Models were estimated using Restricted Maximum Likelihood (REML). The random-effects structure included participant-level random intercepts and random slopes across months:
Y_ij=β_0+β_1 (〖"Group" 〗_i )+β_2 (〖"Month" 〗_ij )+β_3 (〖"Group" 〗_i×〖"Month" 〗_ij )+u_0i+u_1i (〖"Month" 〗_ij )+ε_ij

(■(u_0i@u_1i ))∼N((■(0@0))ⓜ,(■(σ_(u_0)^2&σ_(u_0 u_1 )@σ_(u_0 u_1 )&σ_(u_1)^2 )) ), ε_ij∼N(0ⓜ,σ_ε^2 )

Degrees of freedom and corresponding p-values were calculated using Satterthwaite’s approximation.
R Syntax (lme4 / lmerTest):
                                            content_copy                        Rnote_addویرایش با Canvas
library(lme4)
library(lmerTest)

# Linear Mixed-Effects Model for Speech Production Latency (ms)
lmm_latency <- lmer(
  Latency ~ Group * Month + (1 + Month | Participant),
  data = dataset,
  REML = TRUE
)
summary(lmm_latency)

# Linear Mixed-Effects Model for Conceptual Fluency (1-10 Scale)
lmm_fluency <- lmer(
  Fluency ~ Group * Month + (1 + Month | Participant),
  data = dataset,
  REML = TRUE
)
summary(lmm_fluency)

# Linear Mixed-Effects Model for N400 Amplitude (μV)
lmm_n400 <- lmer(
  N400_Amp ~ Group * Month + (1 + Month | Participant),
  data = dataset,
  REML = TRUE
)
summary(lmm_n400)
